From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: subarr-subgen <subarr@localhost>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 19:21:04 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] 0050: advertise the output-language configuration

Whisper writes exactly ONE subtitle language per job, and subarr could not
see which. In translate mode the output is always English regardless of the
file; in transcribe mode it is the file's own audio language. Neither was
visible in the capabilities block.

The consequence is a loop where both sides are behaving correctly. subarr
sees a row wanting Spanish with no Spanish subtitle and queues it. subgen,
which in translate mode can only ever write English, sees the English
subtitle it would have produced already sitting on disk and refuses. The row
is queued again on the next walk, refused again, forever. Reported as
coaxk/subarr#505 by AztecGuyGDL, who measured about fifteen files cycling on
a fifteen minute schedule.

Emitted as the raw settings rather than a resolved language, because in
transcribe mode the producible language is per-FILE and only subarr knows the
audio language of the row it is weighing. Together with the existing
per_request_task capability, subarr can derive the full producible set:
English via translate, or the file's own audio language via transcribe.

subtitle_language_name is included because it renames the OUTPUT FILE without
changing the language of its contents. An install in translate mode with
SUBTITLE_LANGUAGE_NAME=es writes English text into a .es.srt, and subarr
should be able to see that contradiction rather than trust the filename.
---
 subgen.py | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/subgen.py b/subgen.py
index beea7ed..77d5e87 100644
--- a/subgen.py
+++ b/subgen.py
@@ -842,6 +842,29 @@ def queue_status():
             "skip_audio_languages": [
                 c for c in (lc.to_iso_639_1() for lc in skip_audio_languages) if c
             ],
+            # [v4.29 PATCH #505] What this instance can PRODUCE, as distinct
+            # from what it will skip. Whisper writes exactly ONE language per
+            # job: in translate mode that is always English no matter what the
+            # file contains, and in transcribe mode it is the file's own audio
+            # language. subarr could see neither, so it would queue a row
+            # wanting Spanish at an English-only instance forever -- subarr
+            # correctly sees Spanish missing, subgen sees the English subtitle
+            # it would have written already present and refuses, and nothing
+            # connects the two (coaxk/subarr#505, reported by AztecGuyGDL).
+            #
+            # Emitted as the raw setting rather than a resolved language,
+            # because in transcribe mode the answer is per-FILE and only
+            # subarr knows the audio language of the row it is considering.
+            # Combined with per_request_task above, subarr can work out the
+            # full producible set: English via translate, or the file's own
+            # audio language via transcribe.
+            "transcribe_or_translate": transcribe_or_translate,
+            # Renames the OUTPUT FILE without changing the language of its
+            # CONTENTS. An install in translate mode with
+            # SUBTITLE_LANGUAGE_NAME=es writes English text into a .es.srt,
+            # so subarr needs this to spot that contradiction rather than
+            # trusting the filename it is about to be handed.
+            "subtitle_language_name": subtitle_language_name or "",
             # [PATCH 0027 / #17] detect_language_robust accepts ?track=N (0-based
             # audio stream) so subarr can language-detect a specific non-default
             # track -- gates the Review "re-run on track N" confirmation UI.
